GUI modernization, Phase 5 (sixth slice): Settings' sticky save/discard
bar and unsaved-changes guard - execution order §9 Phase 5, the part of
the Settings line that does not depend on resolving which of the six
named groups are worth building yet (see the progress log's own note on
that separate, still-open question).

## 1. What was built

`useUnsavedChangesGuard(dirty, confirmMessage)` (new, in `Settings.tsx`):
two distinct kinds of "leaving a dirty form" - closing or reloading the
tab (`beforeunload`, the browser's own native dialog, not reimplemented)
and in-app navigation via the hash router, which `beforeunload` never
fires for at all since the document itself never unloads. The second
gets its own guard: on `hashchange`, if the hash actually changed and the
form is dirty, ask `window.confirm`; declining reverts the hash back
(cancelling the navigation), accepting lets it through. A `revertingRef`
exists only so the programmatic revert does not itself re-trigger the
guard it is part of.

The sticky bar itself replaces the previous always-rendered (merely
disabled-when-clean) Save button: now absent entirely until `dirty`,
then a `position: sticky; bottom: 0` bar inside `.workspace-body` (the
shell's one scroll region, ADR 0003) with both "حفظ"/Save and the new
"تجاهل التغييرات"/Discard button, plus a plain-language "you have
unsaved changes" label.

## 2. A real product gap found while designing the test for this

Testing "does the bar appear when a field changes" turned out to require
first discovering that, outside the Tauri shell (this session's only
directly-testable context, and the default state a fresh install lands
in), Settings has exactly one editable, dirty-able control: demo/live/
bundle source radios, where live and bundle are both `disabled={!inShell}`
(bundle also needs a non-empty `bundlePath`), and demo is the default
selection. Starting from the true default state, there is *nothing a
user outside the shell can click to dirty the form at all* - confirmed
by reading `sourceOption`'s enabled conditions directly, not assumed.
This is not a bug this slice introduces (the same gating existed before
it), but it is worth recording: the sticky bar and its guard are real and
correctly built, and this session's own verification of them (both
automated and live-browser) had to start from a non-default *saved*
value (e.g. `kind: "live"`) specifically to have anything to dirty and
revert - the same condition a real user would be in after having
actually used the live source once, then revisiting Settings.

## 3. Full regression

    $ npx tsc --noEmit                             clean
    $ npm test                                     13 files, 106 tests passed
    $ npm run build                                clean
    $ npx playwright test                          37 passed (0 failed, unchanged)

## 4. Proven, not just passing once

15 tests. The two most load-bearing behaviours were each deliberately
broken and confirmed to fail before being restored - not just asserted
and trusted:
- Commenting out the `window.location.hash = guardedHash` revert line:
  the "reverts on decline" test failed with the exact wrong hash value
  (`#/incidents` instead of `#/settings`), confirming the test actually
  depends on the revert happening, not on some other side effect.
- The `beforeunload` test dispatches a real, cancelable `Event` and
  checks `defaultPrevented` directly - proving the handler actually
  calls `preventDefault()` when dirty and does not when clean, not
  merely that the component renders without throwing.

The hash-guard tests dispatch a genuine `HashChangeEvent` against
`window` and read `window.location.hash` back afterward - behavioural
proof, not an inspection of component internals that could pass while
the real browser-level behaviour was wrong.

## 5. Actually looked at it - including a real false alarm correctly diagnosed

Live browser check surfaced a moment worth recording rather than
glossing over: after seeding `localStorage["netrewind.source"]` to
`{"kind":"live"}` and navigating via the hash router, the save bar did
not appear even after clicking "Demo recording". Before concluding
anything was broken, checked directly: the radio *did* change to demo,
but `settings.kind` (the React state, not the storage value) was still
whatever it had been since this long-lived tab last actually loaded the
page - hash-only navigation does not remount the app or re-read
`readSettings()`, so a `localStorage` edit made after the page was
already running cannot retroactively change already-initialized React
state. Forced a real `location.reload()` (not another hash navigation)
so `readSettings()` ran again from the edited storage; from that point
the whole flow worked exactly as designed: clicking "Demo recording"
showed "لديك تغييرات غير محفوظة" with both buttons; clicking "تجاهل
التغييرات" (Discard) removed the bar and reverted the radio back to
"live" (the saved value, not left on the discarded draft). Console clean
throughout. The native `window.confirm` dialog itself was not exercised
live (unreliable under browser automation in general, and already
proven deterministically by the vitest suite above) - recorded as not
attempted, rather than silently skipped without saying so.

## 6. What remains in Phase 5

Rules "match-count in range" (needs the absolute from/to design decided
in the progress log, not yet implemented); the six-group Settings
regrouping question itself (still open - the sticky bar/guard built here
works with any eventual grouping, so it did not need to wait for that
decision). No new ADR - a pure frontend addition to an existing settings
form, no contract change.
